%I A398975 #19 Sep 18 2026 21:10:45
%S A398975 1,1,1,4,1,1,7,8,1,1,11,3,13,14,3,16,1,1,19,3,21,22,1,1,25,26,3,28,1,
%T A398975 1,31,32,1,1,35,3,37,38,1,1,41,42,3,44,1,1,47,3,49,50,3,52,1,1,55,56,
%U A398975 1,1,59,3,61,62,3,64,1,1,67,3,69,70,1,1,73,74,3
%N A398975 Lexicographically earliest sequence of positive integers such that a(n) equals the number of fixed points within distance a(n) of n.
%C A398975 Except n=2, the fixed points of this sequence are A000069 (the odious numbers).
%C A398975 Except n=2, A010060(n) is the indicator function of the set of fixed points.
%C A398975 Beginning at a(3), the lengths of the runs of the fixed points are A104248 beginning at A104248(2).
%C A398975 Beginning at a(3), the lengths of the runs of the non-fixed points are A143331 beginning at A143331(2).
%H A398975 Eric Fox, <a href="/A398975/b398975.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398975 Eric Fox, <a href="/A398975/a398975.txt">Proof of the sequence's correctness and relationship with odious numbers</a>
%F A398975 a(n) = n*F(n) + (1-F(n))*(1 + 2*F(n-1)*F(n+1)), where F(n) = A010060(n) - [n=2].
%e A398975 a(4) = 4 and there are exactly 4 fixed points n where 4-4 <= n <= 4+4: n = 1, 4, 7, or 8.
%e A398975 a(5) = 1 and there is exactly 1 fixed point n where 5-1 <= n <= 5+1: n = 4.
%e A398975 a(6) = 1 and there is exactly 1 fixed point n where 6-1 <= n <= 6+1: n = 7.
%Y A398975 Cf. A000069, A010060, A104248, A143331
%K A398975 nonn,easy,new
%O A398975 1,4
%A A398975 _Eric Fox_, Sep 14 2026
